3,Uto-Aztecan Language Family - YouTubeGuarijio Language and the Guarijío Indian Tribe (Guarijo ...,https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=3GxMTDGpzbo,"Apr 24, 2017 · Enjoy the videos and music you love, upload original content, and share it all with friends, family, and the world on YouTube. Language: Guarijio is a Uto-Aztecan language of northern Mexico, spoken by about 2500 people in Sonora and Chihuahua. It is an agglutinave language with long words that have many suffixes. What language does Guarijio use? Guarjiio uses OVS word order, which is unusual among the world's language, and is also a tone language. There are two main dialects of the language, Mountain Guarijio (also known as Upland Guarijio, Highland Guarijio, or Guarijío de la Sierra,) and River Guarijio (also known as Lowland Guarijio, Guarijío del Río, or Macurawe.) How many people speak Guarijio? It is spoken by around 2,100 Huarijio people, most of whom are monolinguals. The language has two variants, known as Mountain Guarijio (guarijío de la sierra) and River Guarijio (guarijío del río). Who are Mountain Guarijio speakers? Speakers of Mountain Guarijio self-identify as Warihó and call River Guarijio speakers macurawe or makulái. River Guarijio speakers call themselves Warihío and call Mountain speakers ""tarahumaras"". What is the difference between Guarijio and Mountain speakers? River Guarijio speakers call themselves Warihío and call Mountain speakers ""tarahumaras"". Contact between the two groups is scant and, although the linguistic differences between the two are slight, speakers report that mutual comprehension is difficult.",Huarijío,Uto-Aztecan
